    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d329">

                <pb facs="0499" n="400" sameAs="frus:frus1936v02/pg400"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.5731/151: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Norway</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Patterson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Oslo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-11-17T11:00:00+01:00">November 17, 1936—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 17—9 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>61. The Legation’s despatch No. 214, July 11 last.<anchor n="7" xml:id="d329-f1"
                    /> Mr. Bull-Ovrevik<anchor n="8" xml:id="d329-f2"/> has just written Minister
                    Biddle and has telephoned to me from Bergen to emphasize his views that
                    Norwegian interest in possible reciprocal trade agreement is based on desire to
                    obtain United States market for whale oil, that this interest will subside if
                    negotiations are not initiated and concluded by end of current year since the 50
                    percent reduction in tax possible under Trade Agreement Act<anchor n="9"
                        xml:id="d329-f3"/> will be of practical benefit to Norwegian interests only
                    in year of <pb facs="0500" n="401" xml:id="pg401"/> drought such as 1936. If
                    such prompt action is not possible complete removal of whale oil tax alone will
                    interest Norwegians. Mr. Bull linked importation of American automobiles,
                    Norwegian road building program, whale oil and trade agreement in evident desire
                    to show advantages and also possible disadvantages to American interests which
                    might ensue should trade agreement negotiations not be pushed. Foregoing
                    expression of view telegraphed in view of Mr. Bull’s connection with Foreign
                    Office officials.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Patterson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
